    "summary": "The Emin Minaret (Imin Ta) is 44m/144ft high, making it the tallest in China.  The brick work is a striking example of Islamic architecture.  Named after the heroic Uighur general Emin Khoja, the minaret was financed and built by his second son, Suleman, in 1777-1778 both to commemorate the exploits of his father in defending the unification of China and to show gratitude to the Qing dynasty (1644-1912).  Suleman inherited his father’s rank and role as Turpan’s ruler.\nThe adjacent Su Gong Ta Mosque can accommodate 1,000 people.\nOn Google Earth:\n\nEmin Minaret  42°56'3.53\"N, 89°12'12.33\"E",
